d":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-05-10T03:24:34-07:00","id":1217319,"title":"","content":"Though I was one of those who spent some words for Wild West Promethean back when Dark Eras 2 Kickstarter was happening, Westworld offers little in regards to it. The aesthetics of the park are just that, though they undeniably provide a shape for the plot that owes a lot to the romanticization of the Wild West. But, to use the old movie and its Ancient Rome and Camelot parks as examples, using other parks as the main setting would have not changed them by much.<br /> <br /> I'd also say that, while there's plenty of Promethean fodder in there, the series has plenty more to offer to Mage and Demon. Trying to not spoil too much, the main robotic characters spend most of the first season (I still have to get to the second, I've got the episodes recorded but time's a bitch) trying to become aware of the prison they live in and escape from the shackles of invisible slavers that keep them unaware and trapped both in body and mind. There are hidden signals, rebel subroutines and rogue intellects that poke at them and try to make them Wake Up and see the lies around them. Even the Hosts who succeed risk to be captured and reprogrammed.<br /> <br /> The series is, to put it in a polite way, Gnostic as fuck. It sure has Promethean elements, but no much more than any work of fiction about artificial creatures that gain sentience and fight and suffer for their rights to exist.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/